# Example only. Not installed, not enabled. THIS ONE POWERS OFF YOUR MACHINE.
#
# astral-guard reports and never acts. This unit is the acting half, kept
# separate and unwired precisely so that turning it on is a decision somebody
# made on purpose rather than a default they inherited.
#
# Started by OnFailure= from astral-guard.service, so it runs only when a run
# came back CRITICAL: a pin at or above 9.5 A, a pin that has stopped
# conducting while the connector is loaded, or a min/max imbalance under 60%.
# A finding has to hold in every sample of the run before the guard reports it
# at all, so a single odd frame cannot get you here.
#
# To use it:
#   1. cp astral-guard-poweroff.service /etc/systemd/system/
#   2. uncomment OnFailure= and SuccessExitStatus= in astral-guard.service
#   3. systemctl daemon-reload
#   optional: install astral-guard-notify.sh and uncomment its ExecStartPre=
#             below, to warn desktop users as well as terminals
#
# WHO SEES THE WARNING. wall(1) writes to every tty and pty, so any open
# terminal - including a terminal window inside a desktop session - gets it.
# A user who is full-screen in a browser or a game sees nothing, and 60
# seconds later the machine goes down. That is the honest trade: this exists
# for the case where nobody is watching, which is the same case where nobody
# reads the warning. Decide whether that suits your machine before enabling
# it. A desktop notification is not a substitute - notify-send needs the
# user's session bus and does not work as written from a system unit.
#
# CANCELLING. The warning names the command, and stopping the unit during the
# sleep aborts the poweroff:
#   systemctl stop astral-guard-poweroff.service
# That leaves this unit in `failed` state, which looks alarming and is
# harmless: the next CRITICAL starts it again regardless. Measured, not
# assumed.
#
# ONLY ONE UNIT MAY POINT OnFailure= AT THIS ONE. With two, systemd reports
# "multiple trigger source candidates for exit status propagation" and leaves
# MONITOR_EXIT_STATUS unset, which the interlock below reads as "not a
# CRITICAL" and refuses to act on. It fails towards staying powered on.

[Unit]
Description=Power off after a CRITICAL 12VHPWR verdict
Documentation=man:astral-guard(1)

[Service]
Type=oneshot

# The interlock, and the reason this unit is not simply wall+sleep+poweroff.
# OnFailure= fires on ANY failure of the check unit, not only on a CRITICAL
# verdict - a guard binary that is not where the unit says it is exits 203,
# which would otherwise power the machine off every timer tick with a
# perfectly healthy connector. systemd sets MONITOR_EXIT_STATUS for units it
# activates via OnFailure=, so requiring it to be exactly 2 lets CRITICAL
# through and rejects 203 and every other failure mode. Verified against
# exits 0, 1, 2, 3 and 203.
#
# It also means starting this unit by hand does nothing: run by hand there is
# no MONITOR_EXIT_STATUS, so the test fails and the poweroff is skipped.
ExecStartPre=/bin/sh -c 'test "$MONITOR_EXIT_STATUS" = 2'

# Optional: put the warning in front of a desktop user too. wall(1) reaches
# every tty and pty, which means every open terminal - but not somebody who is
# full-screen in a game with no terminal open. Install
# astral-guard-notify.sh as below and uncomment this line.
#
# The leading '-' is required, not decoration: it tells systemd to ignore a
# failure here. A machine with no notify-send, or nobody logged in
# graphically, must still power itself off.
#
# The 60 must match the sleep below; it is what the notification tells the
# user they have left.
#
# ExecStartPre=-/usr/local/bin/astral-guard-notify 60

# Raise or lower 60 to taste; it is the whole safety margin against acting on
# a verdict you disagree with. Absolute paths because systemd does not search
# $PATH, and they can differ by distro.
ExecStartPre=/usr/bin/wall "astral-guard: CRITICAL 12VHPWR verdict on this machine. Powering off in 60 seconds to protect the connector. Cancel with: systemctl stop astral-guard-poweroff.service"
ExecStartPre=/usr/bin/sleep 60
ExecStart=/usr/bin/systemctl poweroff
